A3 China plans to allow top artificial intelligence companies to buy a limited amount of H200 chips from Nvidia, a sign the country is easing restrictions on the coveted US technology, according to the Information news site.
A3 Chinese officials have informed companies such as Alibaba Group, ByteDance and DeepSeek that they will have permission to purchase some of the processors, which are used to develop AI models, the news site said on July 8 .
The companies need to say how many chips they need – and why – to get approval, according to the Information.
A3 The Chinese government, meanwhile, is still figuring out how many H200 chips to allow the companies to buy, but the total may end up being fewer than 200,000, the Information reported.
A3,B1,B2 Though US President Donald Trump granted Nvidia permission to sell the processors to China in December, the Chinese authorities have been slow to allow the technology into the country.
A3,B2,B3 But soaring demand for AI processors helped spur the Chinese authorities to allow some H200 purchases, according to the Information, citing two unidentified people familiar with the matter.
B1,B3 In May, Nvidia chief financial officer Colette Kress said the H200 chips had yet to generate any revenue for the company in China.
B3 Chinese officials have informed companies such as Alibaba Group, ByteDance and DeepSeek that they will have permission to purchase some of the processors, which are used to develop AI models, the news site said on July 8 .
B3 The companies need to say how many chips they need – and why – to get approval, according to the Information.
B3 “We are uncertain whether any imports will be allowed into the country,” she said.

 E054 {'who': 'NVIDIA', 'what': 'sales of its stand-alone Vera CPU line', 'how_much': '$20 billion', 'period': 'by the end of this fiscal year (FY2027)', 'status': 'forecast'}
A1 The company expects to generate $20 billion in sales by the end of this fiscal year
A2,B2 Nvidia also highlighted a new growth driver: its Vera CPU line, which the company expects to generate $20 billion in sales by the end of this fiscal year, a notable push into a market historically dominated by Intel and AMD.
B2 AI startup Perplexity has also chosen to use Nvidia’s new CPUs for AI agent tasks.
B3 The company posted $82 billion in total revenue, up 85% year-over-year, marking its third straight quarter of accelerating growth.
B3 Data center revenue alone hit $75 billion, up 92% year over year.

